  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「コショウボク」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%B3%E3%82%B7%E3%83%A7%E3%82%A6%E3%83%9C%E3%82%AF",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106361430,
      "text": "コショウボク は、15メートルまで成長するウルシ科サンショウモドキ属の常緑樹である。 実の匂いや形が胡椒と似るが、胡椒とは全くの別種であり、関係はない。 鮮やかなピンク色の実は香辛料の「ピンクペッパー」として販売される。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Schinus molle」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schinus_molle",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1358617271,
      "text": "Schinus molle is an evergreen tree in the cashew family (Anacardiaceae) that grows to 15 m. It is native to an area from the Peruvian Andes to southern Brazil. The bright pink fruits of S. molle are often sold as \"pink peppercorns\", although it is unrelated to black pepper. The word molle in Schinus molle comes from mulli, the Quechua word for the tree. The tree is host to the pepper-tree moth, Bombycomorpha bifascia."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Schinus molle」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schinus_molle#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1358617271,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "The tree reproduces through seed, suckers and cuttings. The seeds have a particularly hard coat and germination rates are greatly improved after they have passed through the gut of birds or other animals. Seeds germinate in spring, with seedlings slow growing until established. The seeds easily germinate under the tree in the existing leaf litter of the mother tree, by the hundreds at once and can easily be transplanted.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
